VGE-owned HDR stars and galaxy
=============================

Status: Phase 1 contracts complete after second review and independent completion audit (2026-10-13). Runtime implementation and acceptance remain open.
Authority: User-approved hybrid HDR direction and refinements (2026-10-13).
Related plan: PBR.PhotometricLighting.todo, shared nighttime illumination work.
Diagnostic evidence: ../artifacts/LightingImbalance/findings.md.
Linked contracts: PBR.PhotometricLighting.Proposal.md (calibrated nighttime celestial
lighting) and PBR.Atmosphere.Publication.md (publication and draw ownership).

Goal and approved constraints
  Replace the native stars/galaxy draw with VGE-owned rendering and shader code.
  Generate a linear HDR galaxy cubemap with a VGE shader during world loading;
  do not reuse the base-game galaxy cubemap or its shader implementation.
  Render individual stars using instanced billboards, limited to a few thousand,
  with the count controlled by the global sky quality level.
  Use a common calibrated celestial source for visible appearance and illumination.
  Reuse atmosphere, GPU resource/publication, storage, exposure and glare owners.
  Preserve the current shader baseline. No millions-of-stars compute rasterizer,
  volumetric galaxy simulation, additional shadow maps or block-light changes.

Research references
  - Smaller than Pixels: Rendering Millions of Stars in Real-Time (2025):
    https://elib.dlr.de/215930/1/egs20251029.pdf
    Reference for angular-area normalization and stable subpixel star filtering;
    its atomic compute rasterizer is not the selected implementation.
  - A Physically-Based Nightsky Model (2001):
    https://graphics.stanford.edu/papers/nightsky/nightsky.pdf
    Reference for consistent source appearance, illumination and atmospheric transport.

Phase 1: Define source, quality and publication contracts
-------------------------------------------------------
☑ Identify the authoritative global sky quality option and its supported levels.
  - Reconcile the existing Atmosphere.SkyLutQuality option with the requested global
    setting before wiring consumers; do not introduce an independent star-quality knob.
  - Record exact per-level star caps, cubemap resolutions and generation budgets.
    Keep the highest cap within a few thousand and lower levels strict subsets.
☑ Specify deterministic star and procedural galaxy inputs.
  - Choose a compact licensed catalog or deterministic generated distribution;
    document direction, magnitude, linear color and stable seed/orientation conventions.
  - Define the galaxy shader's broad band, bulge, dust attenuation and multiscale
    structure. Treat this as an artistic spatial model with calibrated light output,
    not a measured reconstruction of the Milky Way.
  - Split resolved stars from unresolved background without duplicate energy. Account
    for stars omitted at lower quality so quality changes do not change total illumination.
☑ Define physical units, normalization, HDR encoding and conservative source bounds.
  - Convert star magnitudes to integrated photometric contributions and normalize
    their filtered angular footprints. Define the diffuse map's luminance budget.
  - Keep airglow separate; replace only the corresponding existing stellar background
    contribution. Preserve moonlight and avoid adding the old background a second time.
  - Specify scaled floating-point cubemap storage, mip filtering and precision checks.
    Do not apply legacy black subtraction, display-space processing or the generic
    compatibility brightness conversion to generated celestial radiance.
☑ Specify complete publication and failure behavior with existing resource owners.
  - Galaxy generation is a world-loading operation, not per-frame work. Rebuild only
    for relevant quality/source changes, context recovery or shader reload as required.
  - Retain the last complete publication on failed replacement; define first-load
    failure handling without reactivating the native stars/galaxy renderer.

Phase 2: Generate the HDR galaxy during world loading
---------------------------------------------------
☐ Implement a VGE-owned GPU generation program for all six cubemap faces.
  - Generate galaxy structure directly from the agreed deterministic inputs. Do not
    sample native sky textures or copy native shader formulas.
  - Use continuous directional coordinates, filtered procedural detail and consistent
    edge treatment to avoid face seams and aliasing in fine dust/stellar structure.
☐ Allocate the agreed floating-point map, generate seam-consistent linear mips and
  produce the derived lighting representation before publishing the complete resource set.
  - Respect GPU context/thread ownership; bound loading cost, scratch memory and work.
  - Keep resources stable across ordinary frames and release them on world teardown.
☐ Validate deterministic generation, finite nonnegative values, normalization, seams,
  mip energy and preservation of faint gradients through the actual texture format.

Phase 3: Render the instanced stars and owned galaxy
-------------------------------------------------
☐ Upload the bounded star set to a persistent instance buffer and draw instanced quads.
  - Select nested quality subsets deterministically; no per-frame catalog rebuild/upload.
  - Use a normalized footprint with stable subpixel movement. Verify integrated light
    across resolution, field of view, screen position and overlapping star footprints.
  - Define edge clipping and culling conservatively so visible footprints do not pop.
☐ Sample the generated galaxy with a VGE-owned shader and matching sky orientation.
  - Preserve daily celestial rotation and horizon/terrain occlusion through existing
    frame inputs. Compose behind atmosphere, clouds and celestial disks correctly.
☐ Replace the native stars/galaxy draw for the owned renderer lifetime.
  - Use the existing explicit ownership pattern; remove obsolete compatibility patching
    for this draw. Avoid duplicate submissions, broad state invalidation or version bumps.
  - Preserve state tracking, reload behavior and lifecycle disposal contracts.
☐ Route both contributions through existing frame storage, exposure and glare.
  - Apply atmospheric transmittance consistently and only once. Let atmospheric
    radiance and exposure govern daylight visibility without a legacy artistic cutoff.
  - Filter the star core without a second independently boosted halo. Evaluate existing
    glare behavior before changing it; document any further required scope separately.

Phase 4: Reconcile celestial appearance and scene illumination
-----------------------------------------------------------
☐ Derive a cached diffuse-light representation from the same complete celestial source.
  - Include the full source budget regardless of the displayed star subset. Integrate
    with solid-angle and cosine weights; do not infer illuminance from peak texel values.
  - Rotate and attenuate this source consistently with visible sky, using existing
    atmosphere transport/publication owners and appropriate refresh dependencies.
☐ Replace the matching constant stellar background in AtmosphereNightLighting.
  - Keep separately modeled airglow and lunar contributions explicit. Reconcile weather
    attenuation and any approximations so they are neither omitted nor applied twice.
☐ Publish coherent results to standalone PBR and LumOn consumers.
  - Cover terrain, forward items/entities, liquids, reflection/environment sampling,
    water transport and probe/cache consumers where applicable.
  - Preserve interior visibility and AO; update lighting bounds, source identities and
    invalidation dependencies. Do not brighten terrain through emission or arbitrary floors.

Phase 5: Qualification and appearance acceptance
-----------------------------------------------
☐ Run focused CPU/GPU checks through subagents, keeping routine fixtures lightweight.
  - Verify star flux and color, footprint stability, quality-budget invariance, galaxy
    continuity, source-to-lighting integration and transmittance consistency.
  - Verify no overflow or loss of faint gradients at actual storage boundaries, including
    simultaneous daylight/twilight sources and exposure extremes.
  - Exercise world load/unload, failed generation, reload, resize, quality changes and
    complete publication without stale textures or duplicate native rendering.
☐ Measure generation latency, resident/scratch memory and steady-state frame cost.
  - Record hardware, resolution and quality; separate loading cost from runtime cost.
    Confirm galaxy generation does not repeat during ordinary frames. Keep expensive
    timing/capture qualification opt-in rather than part of routine correctness tests.
☐ Obtain user-run appearance acceptance.
  - Check fixed exposure first, then auto exposure: moonless/moonlit nights, twilight,
    lantern comparisons, galaxy gradients, star movement, FOV/resolution changes,
    all quality levels, clouds/horizon, terrain/items/water and LumOn on/off.
  - Assess exposure readability separately from source calibration; do not conceal
    source mismatch by increasing ambient brightness or glare.
☐ Reconcile photometric and atmosphere documentation and independently review completion.
  - Record calibration choices, procedural approximations, quality caps, resources,
    validation receipts and actual visual coverage before marking the work complete.

Completion criterion
  VGE owns star/galaxy resources, generation, shaders and drawing without native sky
  shader code or galaxy imagery. A bounded instanced star set and load-generated HDR
  galaxy share a qualified lighting calibration, preserve smooth gradients and stable
  star energy, meet recorded performance budgets and receive user appearance acceptance.

Resolved source, quality and publication contract (2026-10-13)
-------------------------------------------------------------
This section specifies the design to implement; it does not claim runtime qualification.
It governs the tasks above. Existing runtime behavior remains unchanged until implementation.

Quality and bounded work
  Use Atmosphere.SkyLutQuality as the single VGE sky quality setting: integer 0..3,
  default 0, sanitized by AtmosphereSettings.Sanitize. Keep its JSON key and existing
  atmosphere LUT scaling; update its UI label/tooltip to describe global sky quality
  when wiring the new consumers. No second selector or config migration is needed.
  Confirmed in AtmosphereSettings.cs and configlib-patches.json.

  Level   Instanced stars   Galaxy face size   Max noise octaves   Cubemap with mips
    0          1024              512                  4                 8 MiB
    1          2048             1536                  5                72 MiB
    2          3072             2048                  6               128 MiB
    3          4096             2048                  7               128 MiB

  Memory figures are rounded up for six R11G11B10F faces and a complete mip chain.
  Allow two final-format maps during replacement (retained and candidate), plus one
  RGBA16F scratch map and 8 MiB for source buffers, reduction and lighting data: 40/296/520/520 MiB.
  Resolution revision (2026-10-13): user selected 512 pixels per face for the lowest
  level and 2048 for the highest. Use 512/1536/2048/2048; these replace
  the sizes reviewed originally. Highest-quality resident map storage is bounded by
  128 MiB; the conservative replacement budget is 520 MiB. Reuse/tile scratch where
  possible during implementation; do not assume a full-size scratch map is necessary.
  Qualify allocation support (including non-power-of-two faces and partial edge tiles), peak memory
  and loading time at these revised sizes before accepting the implementation.
  Rasterize six faces with a GLSL 330 generation program through existing resource APIs;
  do not require a new compute capability. Generate at most 128x128 texels per submitted
  tile, seven bounded noise octaves, then bounded mip/reduction passes. Never spin-wait
  on completion. Schedule on the existing render-context owner during world loading.
  Qualification targets: <=1 second aggregate generation GPU time at highest quality,
  <=0.5 ms steady-state at 2560x1440 on the qualification machine. Record that machine;
  these are acceptance targets, not measurements or promises for every GPU. Do not
  silently reduce selected quality on failure. Resize/exposure changes never regenerate.

Deterministic source and orientation
  Choose a generated fictional sky, avoiding external catalog licensing and native assets.
  Source version 1 has exactly 4096 stable IDs, a fixed seed 0x56474531, and independent
  unsigned integer hash streams keyed by (version, ID, purpose); specify the exact hash
  implementation with fixtures when implementing. No world RNG or frame seed is consumed.
  Distribute directions uniformly over the unit sphere using z=1-2u, azimuth=2*pi*v.
  Assign raw magnitude m=-1+8*u^(1/3); sort by increasing magnitude, breaking ties by ID.
  Every quality level selects a prefix of this same list. Use a separate random stream
  to interpolate a bounded warm/neutral/cool linear RGB palette; normalize Rec.709 Y to
  one. Palette endpoints before normalization: (1,.65,.35), (1,1,1), (.55,.70,1).
  This is a controlled artistic distribution, not a real star catalog or spectral model.

  Store directions and the cubemap in one right-handed celestial frame (+Y pole,
  +X zero longitude, +Z positive longitude). Define the procedural galaxy plane tilted
  60 degrees about +X, with its bulge at galaxy longitude zero. Use a normalized
  celestial-to-world rotation derived from calendar time and latitude for both stars
  and map; derive inverse sampling from that same matrix. Do not read matrices produced
  as a side effect of the suppressed callback. Verify installed calendar axes/time
  conventions against fixed reference directions during draw integration. World position
  translation never enters this rotation; no galaxy parallax or per-world seed changes.

  Galaxy shape is positive: a broad Gaussian latitude band (sigma 12 degrees), a
  localized bulge (longitude sigma 25 degrees, latitude sigma 8 degrees), attenuated
  by bounded procedural dust, with direction-space multiscale value noise. Define
  W=.05 + (band + 2*bulge)*D*M, D in [exp(-2),1], M in [.5,1.5]; hence .05<=W<=4.55.
  Band and bulge are each in [0,1]. Evaluate continuous unit directions across faces;
  attenuate noise frequencies beyond the pixel footprint. No native texture lookup,
  photographic input, hard black subtraction or thresholded display-space gradients.
  Normalize a warm-neutral bounded color field to Y=1 using the same palette envelope.

Energy partition and atmospheric treatment
  Retain 0.0008 lux as the chosen clear-reference, orientation-averaged horizontal
  moonless total. Allocate 0.0004 to neutral airglow, 0.0003 to unresolved galaxy and
  0.0001 to the full discrete star set. This split is an explicit VGE artistic calibration,
  not a measured astronomical breakdown or a claim from the papers. No additional floor.
  Reference altitude is 0.001 km, aerosol 1, cloud cover zero. Normalize celestial source
  amplitudes once against the existing transport at this reference, averaged over uniform
  rotations; do not renormalize each night, current orientation, weather or exposure.
  Actual directional sky brightness and horizontal illuminance are allowed to vary.

  For a star, raw integrated illuminance E_i=E0*10^(-0.4*m_i), where
  E0=108000/(206264.806247^2) lux follows the 2025 paper's magnitude/area convention.
  A single shared positive scale brings the complete star set to its declared reference
  budget. Store the resulting E_i and equivalent calibrated magnitude explicitly;
  raw magnitude controls relative brightness and does not claim Earth calibration.
  Normalize the galaxy independently against its declared budget. Reference transport
  responses must be finite and positive; otherwise reject generation, never divide by zero.
  Airglow remains the existing uniform upper-hemisphere approximation at .0004/pi nits,
  already ground-level; do not extinguish it as an extraterrestrial source a second time.

  Visible star pixel radiance is E_i*w_p/Omega_p, with nonnegative footprint weights
  summing to one over the full unoccluded footprint and pixel solid angle Omega_p.
  Use a minimum two-pixel-wide filtered core; retain full-footprint normalization at
  screen/terrain edges (do not renormalize the surviving clipped pixels). Normalize
  before atmospheric transmittance and visibility, which physically reduce received flux.
  Instanced quads use additive linear RGB and no independent brightness-scaled halo.

  The base galaxy has no discrete catalog contribution. For low quality, splat only
  omitted stars into the generated background with a normalized spherical kernel;
  remove exactly those IDs from the instance draw. Keep RGB energy and direction in
  the residual, rather than adding a uniform brightness floor. Use a fixed 2-degree
  kernel, integrated over texel solid angles, with explicit cross-face support.
  Lighting integrates the base galaxy plus all 4096 stars exactly once, independently
  of the display split. Use a quality-independent 64x64-per-face source quadrature
  and analytic star directions through existing atmospheric transport. Cached lighting
  is approximate spatially; target <=1% integrated RGB error against refined quadrature.
  Display residuals and map resolution must preserve source integrated RGB within 1%.

  Apply atmosphere transmission once to visible external sources and to their incident
  environment integral. Reuse existing medium data and publication scheduling, not a
  second atmospheric integrator. Retain the bounded cloud factor 1-.9*coverage once
  for background illumination; reconcile the visible cloud path to avoid applying its
  occlusion twice. Finite-path background scattering remains the documented isotropic
  approximation derived from the same attenuated environment E/pi, not a separate source.
  Remove the previous combined .0008/pi background when activating this complete source;
  retain lunar transport and sun/moon shadow handover unchanged.

HDR representation, bounds and qualification obligations
  Generate and normalize with float32 arithmetic. Store the galaxy plus omitted-star
  residual as R11G11B10F (GL_R11F_G11F_B10F), RGB = physical nits * C, with no alpha.
  This is the user-selected initial format. C is a fixed power
  of two per completed source generation, independent of camera exposure. Choose the
  largest such C with C*B<=32000 for the proven per-channel map bound B; decode once.
  This new resource's own encoding does not change existing atmosphere's fixed 1/16 scale.
  Derive B conservatively from W<=4.55, palette maxima, positive normalization factors
  and the sum of omitted-star E_i times the residual kernel's maximum density. Mips are
  nonnegative solid-angle-weighted averages and cannot increase B. Retain safety clamping
  at storage boundaries, but reject an out-of-domain source instead of relying on clipping.

  Bound billboard radiance by sum(E_i*maxRGB_i)/minimum admitted pixel solid angle;
  derive that minimum from the actual projection/viewport and filtered footprint. Include
  overlap of all admitted stars, galaxy maximum and existing celestial/atmospheric terms
  in frame-lighting admission. A zero/invalid angular area rejects the draw. Never reuse
  the galaxy map bound for star peaks. Use existing defensive scene-storage conversion.
  Required later tests: no NaN/Inf, <=1% integrated energy drift, <=1% red/green and <=2% blue storage error
  for positive map samples >=1e-5 of peak, and absolute error <=1e-7 of peak below that.
  Failure of these criteria is a storage-design issue to resolve before release; no
  broad framebuffer-format or GLSL-version change is authorized by this contract.

Publication and recovery
  AtmosphereModSystem remains the scheduling/composition owner. Separate celestial source
  generation, resource storage and instanced drawing responsibilities within that domain.
  Publish map/mips, instance subset, source quadrature, encoding, conservative bounds and
  source identity as one immutable completed source set. Source identity includes algorithm
  version, seed, quality and calibration. Atmospheric results also identify the source set,
  rotation and admitted medium; commit visible and lighting consumers together at the
  existing pre-scene publication boundary. A pending rotation/medium result retains its
  matching completed source/rotation; never pair new stars with stale illumination.
  Quality changes rebuild off to the side. Reload invalidates pending work; retain valid
  completed resources until replacement succeeds. Context loss invalidates all GPU handles
  and returns to startup behavior. Generation/upload errors discard only the candidate.
  Before first success, supply zero stellar/galactic contribution and no owned star draw;
  keep solar/lunar and the separately defined airglow valid. Suppress native stars for the
  owned renderer lifetime, including failure; log failure and retry only on reload/reset
  or relevant input change. Do not silently fall back to native assets or mismatched light.
  Retire old resources only after outstanding GPU use is safe. World unload cancels work,
  clears publication and disposes resources on the owning context. No per-frame readback.

Phase 1 verification boundary
  This is a design-contract phase. GPU generation, exact hash fixtures, native calendar
  adapter verification, numeric tolerances, timing and appearance are obligations of the
  subsequent implementation tasks, not measurements established by these decisions.
  Traceability: quality -> AtmosphereSettings.cs/configlib-patches.json; source/units ->
  cited 2025 paper section 3.2 and 2001 paper source decomposition; diagnostics ->
  ../artifacts/LightingImbalance/findings.md; transport/publication ->
  PBR.PhotometricLighting.Proposal.md, calibrated nighttime celestial lighting, and
  PBR.Atmosphere.Publication.md, publication/draw ownership. The selected split and
  procedural shape are VGE design decisions, not inferred numerical results from papers.
